=== REASONING ===

Goal: deliver the only opt-in surface for AI (per ADR-015 §I7,
§I9) without bending the F0/F1 architecture.

Key design decisions:

1. Validate endpoint lives outside /api/v1/ai/*.
   The prompt's draft suggested a parallel guard.WrapValidating()
   under /api/v1/ai/_internal/validate-config. We rejected that
   because aivet's invariant is "every /api/v1/ai/* mounts via
   guard.Wrap" — adding a second mount macro would duplicate the
   guard surface (P6 DRY) and require an aivet rule loosening. The
   semantically correct home is /api/v1/settings/ai/validate-config:
   it is a settings sub-resource, requires the same standard auth
   as the rest of /settings, is reachable in OFF mode by design (a
   user opting IN must reach the validator while AI is still off),
   and is invisible to aivet (which only inspects /api/v1/ai/*).

2. Archive policy is pure + unit-testable.
   The settings handler couldn't be unit-tested directly (it owns
   *database.SettingsRepo with no port interface). We extracted
   the archive policy into a pure helper
   `applyAIArchiveOnModeFlip(existing, incoming *models.Settings)`
   which the handler invokes during PUT. The helper is nil-safe on
   both sides, defensively clones the archive map (so mutating the
   source after the call cannot leak), filters out explicit `false`
   entries (only restorable choices get archived), and treats
   off→off as a no-op (preserves existing archive across SPA
   round-trips that send the empty map).

3. Restore is explicit, never silent (ADR-015 §I7).
   The Settings UI surfaces the archive as a "Restore previous
   selection?" panel with separate Confirm and Decline buttons.
   Decline dismisses for the session; Confirm reifies the archive
   as the live `ai_features` map AND issues an immediate save (so
   the archive itself is cleared on the round-trip). No
   auto-restore code path exists.

4. Settings handler stays single-document + transactional.
   The frontend uses partial-merge (`useSaveAiSettings` reads the
   cache, deep-merges the AI patch, submits the full blob) so we
   don't need a new patch endpoint. The server's transactional
   Upsert already gives us "either everything saves or nothing".

5. Per-feature toggles are generated, never hand-listed.
   AIFeatureToggleList iterates AI_FEATURE_IDS from the canonical
   TS registry. Adding a feature in F0's Go registry → aigen
   regenerates features.ts → toggle appears here automatically. A
   regression here is caught by aigen --check in CI.

6. Off-mode redaction extended to AIFeaturesArchived.
   F0 already redacts AIProviderConfig in OFF mode. F2's archive
   key has the same secrecy posture (it implies "what features
   you previously trusted"), so the GET handler nils both. The
   PUT handler preserves both fields across round-trips by
   reading the existing settings and back-filling any nil JSONB
   field that the SPA didn't include — the SPA never has to
   re-supply a redacted field.

7. API key never pre-populates in OFF mode.
   The component's useEffect explicitly defaults `api_key=''` when
   serverMode==='off' even though the server already redacts.
   Belt-and-braces: a future change that loosens the server-side
   redaction must also defeat the client-side guard before a key
   can leak.

8. Migration numbering: 000202 (next free).
   Prompt's allowed-files list cited 000197 but 000201 is taken
   by F0's ai_settings migration. Used the next free integer to
   keep migration ordering linear.

